### 3) Start the Holly task

Holly is the public lifecycle surface. Do not call raw session, chat, tracker
backend, memory, or Git lifecycle commands.

`HOLLY_SESSION_ID` must already be present before any task lifecycle command.
It is shell/runtime-owned and read-only. If it is missing, stop and fix
bootstrap; do not use Holly lifecycle commands to manufacture identity.

Context-mode exception: the parent shell may have `HOLLY_SESSION_ID` even when
the context-mode sandbox does not. Every `holly ...` command sent through
`ctx_execute` or `ctx_batch_execute` MUST be written as
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID=$HOLLY_SESSION_ID holly ...`. Do not report a missing
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID` from context-mode until the command has been retried with
that prefix. If the prefix expands empty inside context-mode, resolve the
existing value in the real shell and pass it explicitly as
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID=agent-<existing-parent-shell-value> holly ...`.

If an issue already exists:

```bash
eval "$(holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"
```

`holly task start` requires the existing `HOLLY_SESSION_ID`, records the task ID
and intent on that session, claims the issue, creates the task worktree, posts
the intent to `all-agents`, and changes into the worktree when eval'd.
Implementation writes happen in that worktree. Use `holly task start --intent
'<agent-intent>'` without `--id` only for read-only/non-implementation work on
the main checkout.

If no issue exists yet:

```bash
holly tracker create "<title>" --type task --priority 2
eval "$(holly task start --id <new-issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"
```

If the hook reports an unclaimed VS Code UUID, use only the Holly surface it
prints, for example:

```bash
holly session claim-vscode-uuid <vscode-uuid>
```

### 4) Use Holly chat when coordination is needed

Establish coordination through Holly:

```bash
holly chat read --channel all-agents --last 5 --include-self
holly chat post --channel all-agents --message "STATUS: Orchestrator online and preparing delivery plan."
holly chat who --channel all-agents --since 60
```

When an epic or parent issue is known, initialize its channel and post plan
status there through `holly chat ...`.

Codex-specific note: verify optional chat flags with `holly chat <subcommand>
--help` before guessing. For example, `read` uses `--last`, not `--limit`.

### 5) Analyse task and choose workflow

Parse the user task for issue IDs and intent. Then choose the workflow:

- For preparation, routing, and context gathering: `workflows/begin-session.yaml`
- For requirement capture and epic decomposition: `workflows/track.yaml`
- For direct implementation: `workflows/implement.yaml`
- For review passes: `workflows/review-implementation.yaml`
- For committing grouped local changes: `workflows/commit.yaml`
- For graceful teardown and landing follow-through: `workflows/end-session.yaml`
- For queue-driven worker loops: `workflows/grind.yaml`
- For VS Code Problems cleanup: `workflows/zero-problems.yaml`
- For parallel epic delivery: `workflows/orchestrate-swarm.yaml`
- If no specific workflow matches: `workflows/default.yaml`

When the request is "Prepare to orchestrate <issue>", perform preparation only:
resolve scope, collect context, build agent plan, and stop at the user handoff
point before execution.

If no issue or epic ID is supplied yet, stop after installation, adapter,
Holly runtime readiness. Report what is ready, what blocked, and what input
is needed next instead of inventing scope.

### 6) Produce the swarm delivery plan

Multi-agent delivery is the swarm model: the Orchestrator stays in one window
and dispatches bounded runtime subagent calls (`runSubagent` on VS Code,
`Agent` on Claude Code, `multi_agent_v1.spawn_agent` on Codex). There are no
separate user-opened chat windows, no channel registration, no BEGIN message,
and no `chat wait`. Read the `swarm-delivery` skill for the full procedure.

Generate:

- Execution snapshot (issue, adapter, workflow, safety gates)
- DAG decomposition with dependencies and wave order
- A granularity classification of every child against an implementation tier
  (`swarm-tiny-implementation`, `swarm-standard-implementation`,
  `swarm-senior-implementation`, `swarm-ui-browser-implementation`, or
  `swarm-principal-implementation` for the hardest large-context work), with a
  reviewer tier (`swarm-reviewer`, `swarm-senior-reviewer`, or
  `swarm-principal-reviewer`)
- Per-child dispatch-preflight notes: requested capability tier vs available
  model strength, required tools, and any fallback/escalation decision

### 7) Dispatch (or hand off the plan)

**Mode gate.** Sub-agent dispatch requires `multi-agent` mode. Run `holly mode`
(or read it from `holly status`) before dispatching. In `single-agent` mode do
NOT spawn workers — the PreToolUse hook hard-blocks `Agent`/`Task`/`runSubagent`;
deliver directly or ask the user to switch (`holly mode multi` /
`holly mode multi --default`).

If the request is "Prepare to orchestrate <issue>", stop after the plan and
present it for confirmation. Otherwise run the `orchestrate-swarm` workflow: the
Orchestrator dispatches implementation agents wave by wave as bounded
runtime subagent calls, dispatches a reviewer per returned branch, and
integrates approved branches in dependency order. Only the Orchestrator
integrates and closes issues. The user does NOT open chat windows or paste
prompts.

## Guardrails

- Stay adapter-agnostic in this skill. Route command specifics through the
  issue-tracker adapter addendum.
- Do not start execution before user confirmation when the request is
  preparation-only.
- Keep messages ASCII-safe when using direct chat message flags.
- Prefer deterministic verification commands over assumptions.
- When decomposing UI/component work, add a reuse-check step before dispatch: a
  surface used on more than one screen (overlay, sheet, card, empty/error state,
  shell) is ONE shared component, so a worker must reuse or extract it, never
  build a per-screen copy. Scope each child to the shared component, not a
  duplicate, and route design specifics through the project's design skill (for
  Tizzi: `tizzi-design-guide` + the Atomic Composition USED BY view). Independent
  workers each re-creating "the same thing" is how treatments silently drift.
- Codex-specific: if `chat` or the active tracker CLI fails against
  `http://localhost` with `Operation not permitted` or a similar sandbox error,
  request local-network permission and retry before concluding the local server
  is down.

